export default function SubstrateBroadeningRpgfPaper() {
    return (
        <PaperLayout slug="substrate-broadening-rpgf"
            title="Substrate-Broadening Retroactive Public-Goods Funding"
            subtitle="Allocating a Fixed Reserve on Adoption Counted as It Happens"
            author="Figaro"
            date="July 2026"
            watermark="Figaro Protocol · Preprint"
            abstract={
                <>
                    <p>
                        The florin, the token of the Figaro protocol, has a fixed supply, and 600 million units of it &mdash; 60% &mdash; are reserved to reward the designers of the clauses, registered agreements and assemblies that processes use. This paper specifies the rule that distributes the reserve. It is released over nine annual periods on a rising schedule: 15% over the first two, 30% over the next three, 55% over the last four. Within a period each registration&rsquo;s share is its score over the period&rsquo;s total, and the score is <Math>{"c^{1/3}d^{2/3}"}</Math>, where <Math>{"c"}</Math> counts the resolved processes that carried it and <Math>{"d"}</Math> the distinct staked sellers behind them. No tag, category or identity weights a score; we prove that any weighting layer is either a function of the same counters or a curatorial judgment supplied from outside them.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Use is counted on chain when it happens, from facts the chain already holds: that the order resolved, and that the registration was committed in the agreement both parties signed. Counting at the event removes the posting, the bond, the challenge and the forum that any after-the-fact reconstruction of adoption would require. Sybil resistance is carried by a live stake in the chain&rsquo;s base currency on both sides of a counted event, and breadth is counted in exactly the unit that stake prices. We derive the consequence: fabricated score costs an outlay linear in the score, at a unit price fixed by the member stake, the withdrawal cooldown, the period length and the gas of one resolved process, and capturing a fraction of a period&rsquo;s budget costs an outlay convex in that fraction and unbounded as it approaches one.
                    </p>
                </>
            }
            references={
                <>
                    <li>Buterin, V., Hitzig, Z., &amp; Weyl, E. G. A Flexible Design for Funding Public Goods. <em>Management Science</em>, 65(11):5171&ndash;5187, 2019.</li>
                    <li>Douceur, J. R. The Sybil Attack. In <em>Peer-to-Peer Systems (IPTPS 2002)</em>, Lecture Notes in Computer Science 2429, pp. 251&ndash;260. Springer, 2002.</li>
                    <li>Kalodner, H., Goldfeder, S., Chen, X., Weinberg, S. M., &amp; Felten, E. W. Arbitrum: Scalable, Private Smart Contracts. In <em>27th USENIX Security Symposium (USENIX Security 2018)</em>, pp. 1353&ndash;1370. USENIX Association, 2018.</li>
                    <li>Optimism. <em>Retroactive Public Goods Funding</em>. Ethereum Optimism blog (Medium), July 2021. https://medium.com/ethereum-optimism/retroactive-public-goods-funding-33c9b7d00f0c (accessed 5 September 2026).</li>
                </>
            }
        >
            <PaperSection title="1. Introduction">
                <p>
                    When a process resolves on a public chain, three things are left there: the commitments the parties signed, the bonds those commitments locked, and the buyer&rsquo;s signature that resolved them. The work, the goods and every passage of value between the parties happened off chain. This paper builds a reward on that residue alone. Before the reward can be stated, the reader needs the mechanism that produces the residue, and we state the slice of it the argument uses.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Figaro is a protocol for trade between strangers. A <em>process</em> is one buyer&rsquo;s trade with one or more sellers; each <em>order</em> in it is one buyer&ndash;seller commitment, signed by both. The <em>Core</em> is the set of contracts that accepts those commitments, holds every bond, and resolves a process when its buyer signs. At commit the buyer bonds twice the payment <Math>{"P"}</Math> and each seller twice the cumulative value <Math>{"G"}</Math> through its order, every payment the process has accumulated so far, its own included. The doubling answers retention: value passes off chain, a party who walks away keeps what is in its hands, and a bond equal to that value would be exactly canceled by the taking. Only the buyer resolves, and resolution is atomic: every seller is paid and every bond refunded at once, or none is.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Three comparisons make cooperation the equilibrium. After performance the buyer strictly prefers resolving, worth 0, to never resolving, worth <Math>{"-P"}</Math>, whatever the seller does. Given that, and the buyer&rsquo;s plan to resolve once performance has occurred and not before, the seller strictly prefers performing, <Math>{"+P"}</Math>, to never performing, at most <Math>{"-G"}</Math>. And given the seller&rsquo;s plan, the buyer keeps the process open before performance rather than resolving at once. The last two are the parties&rsquo; best-response checks against one profile, verified jointly: a fixed point. What matters here is the consequence. A resolution is the buyer&rsquo;s own acceptance, terminal once given, so it is the one event on the chain that reports a trade completed, and the only one an adoption count can rest on.
                </p>
                <p>
                    What a commitment means lives in its <em>clauses</em>. A clause is one reusable term, written once and registered publicly under the hash of its specification, defining a relationship between a buyer and a seller or between two sellers. An <em>agreement</em> is the clauses composed for one order; a <em>registered agreement</em> is that composition with its designer&rsquo;s fills and no values, registered for reuse. An <em>assembly</em> is a list of registered agreements composed into one reusable design of a process, which any buyer may instantiate. The wallet that registers any of the three is its <em>designer</em>. Together they are the protocol&rsquo;s substrate: the trades the protocol can express are exactly the ones its clauses, registered agreements and assemblies describe.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Designing one is public-goods work in the strict sense. A registered clause is non-rival, since one process&rsquo;s use leaves it whole for the next. It is non-excludable, since anyone may compose it into an agreement, and the parties to that order attest under it for themselves. And the protocol&rsquo;s own operation returns nothing to its designer: there is no per-use charge and no revenue path from an attestation or a resolution. A designer whose handoff assembly a hundred unrelated buyers instantiate captures none of the value it creates. This is the canonical under-provision problem, reproduced at the substrate.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Retroactive public-goods funding, proposed by Optimism together with Vitalik Buterin (Optimism, 2021), answers under-provision by rewarding work after its value is observable. The premise is that impact is easier to judge in retrospect than in prospect. The proposal leaves that judgment to a results oracle, a body that decides after the fact which work was useful. This paper replaces the oracle with a rule and its judgment with a count the chain keeps itself, and keeps from that lineage the retroactivity and the discipline of fixing the rule in public before the work it judges is done.
                </p>
                <PaperRun title="Contribution.">
                    We specify a fixed allocation with three properties. (i) It scores registrations by <em>substrate-broadening</em>, the number of distinct value-adders who carried them in resolved processes, uniformly and behind counting rules that close the cheap farming vectors. (ii) It resists Sybil manufacture with a two-sided live stake rather than a cap or a weight, and we derive the cost of fabricated score rather than assert it. (iii) It removes the verification problem rather than solving it, by counting adoption as it happens instead of reconstructing it afterwards.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Organization.">
                    Section 2 states what is allocated, to whom, and under what constraints; Section 3 the objective; Section 4 the counting rules; Section 5 the score. Section 6 proves the reward uniform and places Sybil resistance in the stake, and Section 7 prices it. Section 8 gives the distribution, Section 9 the construction that counts adoption as it happens, Section 10 why reconstruction fails, Section 11 the scope, and Section 12 concludes.
                </PaperRun>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="2. The Allocation Problem">
                <PaperRun title="What is allocated.">
                    The florin reserves 600 million units, 60% of its fixed supply, for designer rewards. Accrual runs in nine annual periods, each with a budget fixed at deployment. The budgets group into three rising tranches: 15% of the reserve across the first two years, 30% across the next three, 55% across the last four, split equally within each group. The nine budgets are therefore 45M, 45M, 60M, 60M, 60M, and then 82.5M four times. The claim unit is the period, not the tranche: the grouping describes how the nine figures were chosen, and the contracts know only periods and their budgets.
                </PaperRun>
                <RpgfScheduleFigure idPrefix="substrate-broadening-rpgf-schedule" />
                <PaperRun title="Why the schedule rises.">
                    The budgets rise because the evidence does. A share is divided by the period&rsquo;s total score, and in the first year that denominator is smallest: the least informative reading of what was adopted, and the most manipulable, since a fixed quantity of fabricated score buys a larger fraction of a small honest total than of a large one (Section 7.3 makes this exact). Placing the largest budgets where the measurement is best aligns the reserve with what it measures, and shrinks the worst case when the mechanism is least able to defend itself. Funding work before the evidence exists is a different job, and the DAO, which holds a separate treasury and spends it by human judgment, is the instrument for it.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Why the period is a year.">
                    The period length answers considerations that pull in opposite directions. Two argue for a short period. A designer must price the lag between doing the work and being rewarded for it, and a multi-year lag is hard to price in a token with no protocol-set price. And the period sets the price of a fabricated identity: a member stake can be withdrawn and placed again under a fresh address, so one unit of stake furnishes about the period&rsquo;s length divided by the withdrawal cooldown in identities, and a shorter period narrows that recycling (Section 7.1). One consideration argues for a long period: the honest total that fabricated score must dilute grows with the window it is measured over. A year meets both. A period&rsquo;s accrual is a meaningful reading of adoption, and a four-week cooldown against an annual period prices each fabricated identity at about a fourteenth of a member stake. The period boundaries and the budgets are one schedule: the reward contract refuses to deploy unless it receives exactly one budget per period, and no party can accelerate, delay or resize any of it afterwards.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="To whom.">
                    The reward goes to designers of record: the wallet that first registered the clause, registered agreement or assembly, for as long as the stake behind that registration stays live (Section 8). Each family is keyed by content, never by name. A clause&rsquo;s key is the hash of its specification&rsquo;s published bytes, so a specification that differs by one byte is another clause, with its own key, designer, stake and adoption; a title is read by people and by nothing in the count. A registered agreement&rsquo;s key, its <em>agreement key</em>, is the merkle root over one leaf per clause it composes, each leaf the clause key and the designer&rsquo;s fills. An assembly&rsquo;s key is the merkle root over one leaf per registered agreement it holds and, when the assembly composes clauses for the whole design, its <em>assembly terms leaf</em>. One resolved process credits the designer of each registered agreement its orders instantiated, the designer of its assembly, and, independently, the designer of every clause committed in it. There is no reward path per attestation, per seller or per resolution: a seller who attests heavily under another designer&rsquo;s clause earns no designer reward, and neither does a buyer who resolves a thousand processes.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Under what constraints.">
                    The rule must add no governance surface, since every adjustable parameter is a point of capture, and no human curation, which would reintroduce the discretion the protocol displaces. It must resist gaming: an objective formula over public data must not reward behavior that is cheap to manufacture and uncorrelated with substrate growth. And it must be verifiable: anyone assessing the token can confirm that each period&rsquo;s budget was allocated by the stated rule, over inputs no allocator chose.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRemark title="Why not prospective or per-use funding.">
                    Two alternatives are excluded. <em>Prospective</em> funding, grants decided before the work by a body that predicts impact, reintroduces the discretion this rule exists to avoid; it belongs to the DAO&rsquo;s treasury, not to the reserve. <em>Per-use</em> funding, a reward on each attestation under a clause, couples issuance to a per-event trigger and invites wash attestation to farm it. A retroactive, formula-based allocation avoids both: impact is observed rather than predicted, and the reward attaches to the durable registration rather than to the event.
                </PaperRemark>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="3. The Substrate-Broadening Objective">
                <p>
                    The schedule fixes how many florins each period distributes, not which designers receive them. That is decided by the objective, and the choice of objective is the substantive design decision. We call it <em>substrate-broadening</em>: a registration broadens the substrate to the extent that it is adopted across distinct trading relationships. A clause that fifty unrelated sellers each adopt has extended the protocol into fifty practices. A clause one seller uses fifty times has deepened one practice. Both are valuable; only the first broadens the substrate, and that is the quantity rewarded.
                </p>
                <p>
                    An objective is only as good as the statistic that stands in for it, and here the statistic is constrained from outside the objective. Breadth must be measured in a unit the mechanism can <em>price</em>, or it can be manufactured and the objective is a wish. The unit is therefore the distinct <em>seller of record</em>, the seller named in a counted order, who holds a live member stake in the chain&rsquo;s base currency. Section 4 shows why the counterparty pair, the obvious unit of a relationship, cannot be priced; Section 7 shows what the seller unit buys.
                </p>
                <PaperRun title="Resolved only.">
                    The objective is measured over work that resolved. A process contributes to a score only once it has resolved, and a registration with no resolved adoption in a period receives nothing. This ties the reward to completed trade rather than declared intent, and it closes the cheapest farming vector, manufacturing commitments that never resolve, because such a commitment cannot be counted at all.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Relation to quadratic funding.">
                    The principle of breadth over volume has an antecedent in quadratic funding (Buterin, Hitzig, &amp; Weyl, 2019), where a public good&rsquo;s total funding is <Math>{"\\bigl(\\sum_i \\sqrt{c_i}\\bigr)^2"}</Math> over individual contributions <Math>{"c_i"}</Math>, the match being that total less the contributions, so that many small contributions outweigh a few large ones. Substrate-broadening applies the intuition on another axis: breadth of adoption, in distinct staked sellers, over depth of use. The resemblance ends there. There are no donors, no matched contributions and no matching pool; the reward is a fixed reserve divided by observed adoption, and its exponents are not quadratic.
                </PaperRun>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="4. The Counting Rules">
                <p>
                    Six conditions decide whether a count lands, and none is a filter applied downstream: an ineligible count never enters the accounting. The count reaches the chain by one of two routes. On the <em>direct</em> route a counting call names one resolved order, and the call checks all six itself: that the order resolved, that the registration was committed in the signed agreement, that the process has not been counted for it before, that the seller of record holds a live member stake, that the registration&rsquo;s own stake is live, and that the key is not one of the format&rsquo;s two reserved keys. On the <em>batched</em> route, a <em>batch</em> is a set of processes resolved together in one transaction on the strength of a validity proof, and its accrual is proved with it. The proof, under a verifying key fixed at deployment, discharges the first three conditions. The counting call checks the other three itself: the seller&rsquo;s member stake and the registration&rsquo;s stake, which are live chain state the proof cannot see, and the reserved keys, format constants the proof already refuses and the call skips again. It also checks the one further piece of live state, that the batch&rsquo;s accrual lands in the period that is open. Three further rules turn admitted counts into a score: what breadth is counted in, the minimum support a registration needs, and how the two routes are scored.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Every rule depends on one property: a count that lands is permanent. The counters, the score and the period&rsquo;s total move when a count is admitted and can only grow from then on. A direct count increments them; a proved batch writes cumulative figures, which are refused if they fall below what is stored. Nothing anywhere retracts a landed count, so every gate below is a gate on the moment of counting.
                </p>
                <ul className="space-y-2 list-disc pl-6 text-sm">
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">Resolved only.</span> The counting call recomputes the order&rsquo;s hash from the signed commitment and asks the Core for its status. An unknown order is refused, and so is an order that has not resolved.</li>
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">Committed in the signed agreement.</span> The count must be proved against the merkle root the parties signed. A clause is proved directly: its leaf, the clause key and the committed bytes, opens under that root, by the same leaf construction attestations use. A registered agreement and an assembly are never leaves of a signed agreement. Each is proved through the agreement&rsquo;s reserved <em>provenance leaf</em>, which names the agreement key the order instantiated and the root of the assembly that holds it; an assembly is proved, in addition, by a <em>membership proof</em> that the agreement&rsquo;s leaf opens under the assembly&rsquo;s root (Section 9). Nobody can credit a registration to a process that did not commit it, because the proof runs against a digest neither party can restate after signing.</li>
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">One process, one count.</span> A process counts once for a registration, once ever, not once per period. A repeat is refused, so the call may safely be attempted by anyone at any time. Because a count names one order, the seller credited is the seller of that order, whichever order of a multi-seller process it is: a process adds one to the process count and at most one seller to the breadth.</li>
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">Seller staked.</span> A process counts only if its seller of record holds a live member stake in the chain&rsquo;s base currency, which is what prices a seller identity. Requesting the stake back removes the seller from view at the request, not at the release, and from then on its resolved trade counts toward nothing, including trade that resolved earlier and was not yet counted. What was counted stands. The practical consequence is a habit: a process is counted at resolution, when its seller is still staked. A seller who withdraws to deny a designer its count must stay unstaked to the period&rsquo;s end, forfeiting its own visibility and waiting out the cooldown, which is why the mechanism carries the exposure rather than machinery against it. On the batched route the exposure is wider: a batch&rsquo;s accrual is proved before it is submitted, so one seller who withdraws in between fails the gate for the whole batch, and the batch&rsquo;s accrual is dropped for every registration and seller it carried. The trade still resolves, since a reward gate never unwinds a trade. The dropped accrual is recovered by the next batch that touches the same registrations, because batches write cumulative figures, or is forgone if none follows while the period is open. The error runs one way: under-reward, never over-reward. The buyer side is ungated: anyone may buy, and a buyer&rsquo;s purchase counts toward a designer&rsquo;s reward without the buyer staking anything.</li>
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">Registration live.</span> The registration a count names must hold a live stake at that moment, in the registry of its own family &mdash; clauses, registered agreements or assemblies &mdash; and never another&rsquo;s. Without this gate a self-written agreement could commit any 32-byte key and accrue score to it for the gas alone, inflating the denominator every honest designer is rewarded from.</li>
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">The reserved keys earn nothing.</span> The format reserves two keys, each the hash of a fixed label rather than of any specification: the provenance leaf of a signed agreement, and the assembly terms leaf of an assembly&rsquo;s root. Neither is a term the parties chose. The provenance leaf is the format&rsquo;s own statement of which registered agreement and assembly the order instantiated, and the terms leaf is the container for an assembly&rsquo;s whole-design clauses. Both are refused on every kind: a direct count naming either reverts, the batch&rsquo;s proof refuses a claim on either, and the counting call skips either in any batch it receives. The agreement registry refuses either key as a registration. The clause and assembly registries take any non-zero key, so a wallet can stake one of these keys there, and the count disregards that stake. Under the hash function&rsquo;s second-preimage resistance neither key collides with a clause key. The line is structural, between a term and the format that carries the terms, and it reads no content.</li>
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">Breadth counts distinct staked sellers.</span> A seller who carries the same registration repeatedly within a period adds to the process count each time and to breadth once. Breadth is counted in exactly the unit the stake prices: one unit requires one live staked seller, and within a route no arrangement of wallets returns more than one unit per identity. Distinct (buyer, seller) pairs would be the obvious unit, but pairs cannot be priced. The buyer side holds no stake, so one staked seller trading with <Math>{"N"}</Math> fresh buyer wallets would manufacture <Math>{"N"}</Math> units of breadth for the gas alone. Staking both sides does not repair it: <Math>{"k"}</Math> staked buyers trading with <Math>{"m"}</Math> staked sellers mint <Math>{"k \\cdot m"}</Math> pairs from <Math>{"k + m"}</Math> stakes, a quadratic return on what is staked. Distinct staked sellers is the statistic with no leverage, and Section 7 rests on that property.</li>
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">Minimum support.</span> A registration scores nothing in a period until a floor number of distinct staked sellers, three at the reference deployment, have carried it there. The floor withholds scoring, never counting: the counters accrue below it, and the full score appears when the third seller lands. It filters the thinnest entries, the ones one or two identities stand behind: a shard split off an existing clause, a copied specification registered ahead of its designer, a trivial rider committed beside real work. It does not make them impossible. Three staked identities and three resolved processes are a price, not a barrier, and Section 7.3 quotes it.</li>
                    <li><span className="font-semibold text-ink-heading">One floor per route.</span> The counter keeps a separate pair of counters for each route, scores each route on its own, and sums the two scores; the floor applies to each route&rsquo;s own tally. The reason is that the routes present different things. A direct count names its seller, so the counter holds that route&rsquo;s seller set and can tell a repeat from a new seller; a batch writes cumulative counts without the seller sets behind them. The counter cannot take a union it does not hold, so it floors each route apart, which can only under-reward support genuinely split across both. Summing scores has a bounded bias the other way. A seller active on both routes enters each route&rsquo;s breadth, so with <Math>{"n"}</Math> sellers carrying <Math>{"n"}</Math> processes on each route the sum is <Math>{"2n"}</Math> against a pooled <Math>{"(2n)^{1/3}n^{2/3} \\approx 1.26\\,n"}</Math>. The factor is at most <Math>{"2^{2/3} \\approx 1.59"}</Math>, attained at an equal split, because the score is homogeneous of degree one and concave in the process count, and it is bought at the same live stake per seller as any breadth.</li>
                </ul>
                <PaperRun title="What the count does not interpret.">
                    Every rule is decided by comparing hashes, reading a status, reading a stake, or comparing a counter with a threshold. No participant has to agree with a curator about what a clause <em>means</em> to agree about what it scored. The count reads no content and consults no approval list, so a registration carried by many resolved processes and many staked sellers scores for that fact alone.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRemark title="Why the clauses every order carries earn.">
                    Two clauses ride every order: the one stating the consideration and the one stating the order of the sellers. Their counters therefore track counted resolution itself, and scoring them rewards whichever wallet registered them, on every counted process. That wallet is decided by registration, which is permissionless and first-write-wins; the counter reads a registrant&rsquo;s address and never an identity. At the reference genesis the two are registered under the DAO&rsquo;s treasury wallet, because the treasury develops them, so the DAO is rewarded from the reserve, on the same uniform rule, for the use of the clauses it holds. Two properties follow with nothing tuned for them. The DAO&rsquo;s share dilutes itself in the right direction: those counters can do no better than full coverage, while every other scoring registration enters the same total, so its share falls as the set of designers broadens and is largest where little else emerged. And what it accrues outlasts the reserve: nothing accrues after the ninth period, while the DAO spends what it holds on its own judgment and its own clock. A competing clause registered by any other wallet is scored on the same counters, and any agreement may commit it.
                </PaperRemark>
                <PaperRun title="Counting is permissionless and opt-in.">
                    Anyone may submit a count; nothing is trusted about the caller, because the proof is what is checked. In practice the caller is the party with the incentive, the designer, and the gas is the designer&rsquo;s. An uncounted resolution does not count. Its evidence stays on chain, but a count lands in whichever period is open when it is made, and a closed period admits nothing further. Counting must therefore be current, and its cost falls on the party it benefits.
                </PaperRun>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="5. The Scoring Formula">
                <p>
                    Each registration <Math>{"a"}</Math> carries, per period, one pair of counters for each route: <Math>{"(c_1(a), d_1(a))"}</Math> for the direct route and <Math>{"(c_2(a), d_2(a))"}</Math> for the batched route. Its score is the sum of the two route scores, and the period&rsquo;s total moves by the change in a score whenever a count lands, so no pass over history is ever required:
                </p>
                <div className="my-3 overflow-x-auto">
                    <Math display>{"\\text{score}(a) \;=\; \\sigma\\bigl(c_1(a), d_1(a)\\bigr) + \\sigma\\bigl(c_2(a), d_2(a)\\bigr), \\qquad \\sigma(c, d) \;=\; \\begin{cases} c^{\\,1/3}\\, d^{\\,2/3}, & d \;\\ge\; d_{\\min},\\\\[4pt] 0, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{cases}"}</Math>
                </div>
                <ul className="space-y-1 list-disc pl-6 text-sm">
                    <li><Math>{"c"}</Math> &mdash; the number of distinct resolved <em>processes</em> counted for the registration on that route in the period.</li>
                    <li><Math>{"d"}</Math> &mdash; the number of distinct live-staked <em>sellers of record</em> across those processes, each counted once however often it trades in the period.</li>
                    <li><Math>{"d_{\\min}"}</Math> &mdash; the minimum-support floor, three at the reference deployment, applied to each route&rsquo;s own pair.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>
                    On each route <Math>{"d \\le c"}</Math> by construction, since every counted seller is the seller of a counted process, so a route that meets the floor has <Math>{"c \\ge d_{\\min}"}</Math> as well. There is no weight term. Every registration is scored by the same function of its own counters, and Section 6 proves that no weight could be added without either restating that function or importing a judgment.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The exponents are constitutive of the mechanism: breadth carries <Math>{"\\tfrac23"}</Math> and volume <Math>{"\\tfrac13"}</Math>, fixed at deployment for every registration. The effect is easiest to read in the form <Math>{"\\sigma = d\\,(c/d)^{1/3}"}</Math>: a registration scores its distinct staked sellers, multiplied by the cube root of its average repeat depth. Eight times the depth earns twice the score, and where every seller carries one process (<Math>{"c = d"}</Math>) the score is the seller count itself. The reading behind the exponents is that a new adopter tells more about substrate growth than another observation of a known one; that is a judgment about adoption, offered as one. The exponents are not a Sybil defense and cannot be one. No scoring function distinguishes a fabricated seller from a genuine one, since any concavity that dampens the first dampens the second identically. Resistance lives in the price of an identity (Sections 6 and 7), which is why the statistic under the larger exponent had to be one the stake prices.
                </p>
                <PaperRun title="Integer arithmetic.">
                    The score is computed in integers. Above the floor each route&rsquo;s score is <Math>{"\\lfloor\\sqrt[3]{\\,c\\,d^{2}\\cdot 10^{18}}\\,\\rfloor"}</Math>, an integer cube root over a fixed-point scale large enough that the flooring error is negligible. The score is stored, and when a count moves <Math>{"c"}</Math> or <Math>{"d"}</Math> the period&rsquo;s total moves by the new score less the stored one. There is no drift to reconcile, and a reader mirroring the same operations obtains the identical number. The scale multiplies every score by the same constant and cancels from every share, so Section 7 works in the unscaled quantity <Math>{"(c\\,d^{2})^{1/3}"}</Math>.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Value is excluded.">
                    Neither payment nor bond size enters the formula. A clause that carries many small resolutions has done the same substrate work as one used in a few large ones. Weighting by value would import the &ldquo;total value locked&rdquo; metric of financial protocols, and it would skew the reward toward whoever designs the clauses high-value trades happen to use, which is uncorrelated with how broadly a clause extends the substrate.
                </PaperRun>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="6. Uniform Reward and the Two-Sided Stake">
                <PaperRun title="Why the reward is uniform.">
                    A weighting scheme, rewarding more for the category of work the substrate is judged to need, requires the mechanism to make and keep current a standing judgment about which category that is. That judgment is curation in arithmetic form. It privileges a class of work ahead of the evidence, it owes a defense to every designer whose work it rewards less, and it is the discretion the rest of the mechanism refuses. Uniformity declines to make it: every registration&rsquo;s score is its own use alone, and what participants actually adopted decides the split.
                </PaperRun>
                <p>
                    A referee may ask whether a weight is only arithmetic, and arithmetic is not discretion. The question has an exact answer. Write <Math>{"A"}</Math> for the registrations a period admits, and <Math>{"\\kappa(a) = \\bigl(c_1(a), d_1(a), c_2(a), d_2(a)\\bigr)"}</Math> for the counters of Section 5, which are everything the count holds of <Math>{"a"}</Math>. Call a <em>weighting layer</em> any map <Math>{"w : A \\to \\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}"}</Math> under which a registration is rewarded a share proportional to <Math>{"w(a)\\,\\text{score}(a)"}</Math>. The uniform rule is <Math>{"w \\equiv 1"}</Math>.
                </p>
                <FormalBlock label="Proposition (A weighting layer is inert or discretionary).">
                    <p>
                        Let <Math>{"w"}</Math> be a weighting layer. Exactly one of the following holds. (i) <Math>{"w"}</Math> factors through the counters: there is a function <Math>{"W"}</Math> with <Math>{"w(a) = W\\bigl(\\kappa(a)\\bigr)"}</Math> for every <Math>{"a \\in A"}</Math>, in which case the weighted rule is itself a uniform rule, scoring every registration by the single function <Math>{"W(\\kappa)\\,\\text{score}(\\kappa)"}</Math> of its own counters. (ii) Two registrations with identical counters carry unequal weights, in which case <Math>{"w"}</Math> ranks them by something other than their use: their family, their key, or a quantity the count does not hold. Such a ranking may be computable from what the count admits once it is given, but nothing the count admits chooses it; it is a judgment of which work deserves more, supplied from outside the count and maintained there.
                    </p>
                </FormalBlock>
                <Proof>
                    The dichotomy is exhaustive: either <Math>{"\\kappa(a) = \\kappa(b)"}</Math> implies <Math>{"w(a) = w(b)"}</Math> for all <Math>{"a, b \\in A"}</Math>, or it does not. Suppose it does. Then <Math>{"W(\\kappa) := w(a)"}</Math> for any <Math>{"a"}</Math> with counters <Math>{"\\kappa"}</Math> is well defined, and the share rewarded to <Math>{"a"}</Math> is proportional to <Math>{"W\\bigl(\\kappa(a)\\bigr)\\,\\text{score}\\bigl(\\kappa(a)\\bigr)"}</Math>: one function of the counters, applied to every registration alike, which is (i). Suppose it does not, and fix <Math>{"a, b"}</Math> with <Math>{"\\kappa(a) = \\kappa(b)"}</Math> and <Math>{"w(a) \\neq w(b)"}</Math>. Of a registration the rules of Section 4 admit its key, its family, the resolved processes counted for it and the distinct live-staked sellers behind them, and nothing else: no content is read, no category is held, no property of the registrant enters. Since <Math>{"a"}</Math> and <Math>{"b"}</Math> agree on the counters, what separates them is their family, their key, or nothing the count holds. In the last case <Math>{"w"}</Math> is not computable from the count at all. In the first two it is, since every count is kept by family and key, but the count supplies only the labels, never an order among them: that clauses earn twice what assemblies earn, or that one key earns more than another with the same use, is a category judgment that no counter determines. Either way the separating value enters as an input from outside, supplied by whoever holds it, revised as adoption moves, and owed a defense to every designer it rewards less: a standing curatorial judgment, which is (ii). <Math>{"\\square"}</Math>
                </Proof>
                <p>
                    Neither branch does what a weighting scheme is proposed to do. Branch (i) is a restatement: a content-blind reweighting is a different function of the same counters, which is where the exponents of Section 5 already sit, argued in the open and fixed before the work they judge. Branch (ii) is curation, and a scheme that rewards the category of work the substrate is judged to need is branch (ii) by construction. The reserved keys of Section 4 are not a third case: they are refused before a count lands and never enter <Math>{"A"}</Math>.
                </p>
                <PaperRun title="Where Sybil resistance goes: the two-sided live stake.">
                    With no weight to tune and no cap to bind, what stops the counts being manufactured is a stake in the chain&rsquo;s base currency, required live on both sides of a counted event. On the seller side, a process counts only if its seller of record holds a live member stake when the count lands, and since breadth is counted in such sellers, this one gate prices the breadth term directly. On the designer side, the registration must hold a live stake for a count naming it to land, and its designer must still hold that stake when the closed period is claimed (Section 8). The two gates read at different moments. The seller&rsquo;s stake governs what may still be counted and nothing else, since a landed count is permanent. The designer&rsquo;s stake is read at each count and again at the claim, so withdrawing before the claim forfeits the whole period, adoption already counted included. Neither stake is spent, and neither is returned by the reward.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="The stake is exposure to the network, not a cost.">
                    A required stake reads naturally as a cost to minimize, but its denomination says otherwise. The stake is placed in the chain&rsquo;s base currency, the unit gas is spent in, and demand for that unit rises with the trade the substrate carries: more resolved processes mean more transactions, and more transactions mean more demand for gas. A participant who stakes holds exposure to the growth its own work helps produce. That appreciation, if it comes, aligns everyone who stakes, a Sybil riding on others&rsquo; growth included, so it aligns the honest majority rather than deterring the adversary; the deterrent is the price Section 7 derives. The two units the mechanism touches are divided by function. The reward is minted in the florin, from the reserve; the stake is placed in the base currency. The coupling runs one way. No resolution mints, consults or is denominated in the florin by rule: a process is denominated in whatever ERC-20 token its parties chose, the florin among them, and nothing prefers or rewards that choice. The reward mints florins by reading what resolution left, and nothing it counts, scores or rewards moves a bond or any party&rsquo;s position in an open process. A reward that could move a bonded position would be a party to the equilibrium of Section 1; this one only reads its outcome.
                </PaperRun>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="7. The Sybil Bound">
                <p>
                    A mechanism that distributes value on a public count owes its readers a price list: how much fabricating adoption costs, in what unit, and how the cost grows with the share an adversary wants. The setting is the one Douceur (2002) named. Identities are free to create in an open system, so a mechanism that counts them must tie each to a scarce resource or accept that its count can be manufactured. This mechanism ties them to a stake, and what follows is the exchange rate between that stake and the reward. It rests on one property from Section 4, that a unit of breadth costs one live staked seller and never less, and on nothing about the florin&rsquo;s price, which the design does not set.
                </p>
                <PaperSubsection title="7.1 The two costs an adversary faces">
                    <p>
                        Write <Math>{"D"}</Math> for the member stake a seller of record must place, <Math>{"T"}</Math> for the cooldown between requesting it back and receiving it, and <Math>{"P"}</Math> for the length of a period. Eligibility ends at the <em>request</em>, so one stake can be run through a sequence of identities within a period: register a fresh address, resolve and count with it, request the stake, wait out the cooldown, register the next. One stake furnishes at most <Math>{"\\lceil P/T \\rceil"}</Math> identities in a period, and the outlay of holding one fabricated staked seller for one period is
                    </p>
                    <div className="my-3 overflow-x-auto">
                        <Math display>{"\\delta \;=\; \\frac{D}{\\lceil P/T \\rceil} \;\\approx\; D\\,\\frac{T}{P}\\,, \\qquad \\text{saturating at } \\delta = D \\text{ once } T \\ge P."}</Math>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        A cooldown of zero makes <Math>{"\\delta"}</Math> vanish, because one stake then serves unboundedly many identities in sequence. A cooldown of a period or more makes each fabricated identity cost the full stake, the strongest the pair can be. The stake and the cooldown are one price, and neither means anything alone.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Write <Math>{"g"}</Math> for the marginal gas of one fabricated resolved process: its commit and resolve on the cheapest route available, plus the counting call or its share of a batch&rsquo;s accrual. It is a measured constant of a deployment, not a modelling assumption, and it depends on the route: a proved batch spreads one proof&rsquo;s verification across many orders and carries no per-order counting call, so its gas per order is lower than resolving one order at a time. That is why <Math>{"g"}</Math> must be measured on the route an adversary would actually use. We credit the adversary with nothing else. A fabricated process must also lock and release bonds and clear the protocol&rsquo;s non-zero payment floor, and we do not count the time value of either, so the bound errs in the adversary&rsquo;s favor. One convention makes the two costs addable: <Math>{"\\delta"}</Math> is committed for a period and reclaimable at its end, <Math>{"g"}</Math> is spent outright, and both enter the outlay <Math>{"C"}</Math> at face value in the base currency. A reader who prices the waiting applies their own time value to the <Math>{"\\delta"}</Math> term alone, which moves the level of every figure below and the shape of none.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        A reader needs four constants and one measurement: the member stake <Math>{"D"}</Math> and the cooldown <Math>{"T"}</Math>, which price a fabricated seller; the period length <Math>{"P"}</Math>; the designer-side registration stakes, which price a fabricated registration (Section 7.3); and <Math>{"g"}</Math>, held by no contract and measured on the route in question. No florin price enters.
                    </p>
                </PaperSubsection>
                <PaperSubsection title="7.2 The cost of fabricated score">
                    <p>
                        Consider an adversary who wants score <Math>{"s"}</Math> on one route for a registration it designs, and who chooses how much to buy with identities and how much with volume. With <Math>{"n"}</Math> fabricated staked sellers and <Math>{"c"}</Math> fabricated resolved processes the score is <Math>{"s = (c\\,n^{2})^{1/3}"}</Math>. Every seller needs at least one process, so <Math>{"c \\ge n"}</Math>, and the floor requires <Math>{"n \\ge d_{\\min}"}</Math>. The adversary&rsquo;s problem is
                    </p>
                    <div className="my-3 overflow-x-auto">
                        <Math display>{"\\min_{n,\\,c}\;\; C \;=\; n\\,\\delta \;+\; c\\,g \\qquad \\text{subject to} \\qquad (c\\,n^{2})^{1/3} = s, \\quad c \\ge n \\ge d_{\\min}."}</Math>
                    </div>
                    <FormalBlock label="Proposition (Unit price of fabricated score).">
                        <p>
                            For <Math>{"s \\ge s_{0}"}</Math>, where <Math>{"s_{0} = d_{\\min}\\max\\{1,\\,(\\delta/2g)^{1/3}\\}"}</Math>, the minimum outlay of fabricating score <Math>{"s"}</Math> is <Math>{"C^{*}(s) = \\gamma \\, s"}</Math>, where
                        </p>
                        <div className="my-3 overflow-x-auto">
                            <Math display>{"\\gamma \;=\; \\begin{cases} \\delta + g, & \\delta \\le 2g,\\\\[4pt] 3\\cdot 2^{-2/3}\\,\\delta^{2/3}g^{1/3} \;\\approx\; 1.89\\,\\delta^{2/3}g^{1/3}, & \\delta > 2g.\\end{cases}"}</Math>
                        </div>
                        <p>
                            The outlay is <em>linear in the score fabricated</em> in both regimes, and the two expressions agree at <Math>{"\\delta = 2g"}</Math>, where both equal <Math>{"3g"}</Math>.
                        </p>
                    </FormalBlock>
                    <Proof>
                        Relax <Math>{"c \\ge n"}</Math> and substitute the score constraint <Math>{"c = s^{3}/n^{2}"}</Math>, giving <Math>{"C(n) = n\\delta + g\\,s^{3}/n^{2}"}</Math>. This is strictly convex on <Math>{"n > 0"}</Math>, and <Math>{"C'(n) = \\delta - 2g\\,s^{3}/n^{3} = 0"}</Math> yields the interior optimum <Math>{"n^{*} = s\\,(2g/\\delta)^{1/3}"}</Math>, hence <Math>{"c^{*} = s\\,(\\delta/2g)^{2/3}"}</Math>. Feasibility of the relaxed optimum requires <Math>{"c^{*} \\ge n^{*}"}</Math>, i.e. <Math>{"(\\delta/2g)^{2/3} \\ge (2g/\\delta)^{1/3}"}</Math>, which holds exactly when <Math>{"\\delta \\ge 2g"}</Math>. In that regime, substituting back gives <Math>{"C^{*} = s\\,\\delta^{2/3}\\bigl[(2g)^{1/3} + g\\,(2g)^{-2/3}\\bigr] = s\\,\\delta^{2/3}g^{1/3}\\,2^{-2/3}(2+1) = 3\\cdot 2^{-2/3}\\,\\delta^{2/3}g^{1/3}\\,s"}</Math>. When <Math>{"\\delta < 2g"}</Math> the relaxed optimum violates <Math>{"c \\ge n"}</Math>. Along the constraint curve <Math>{"c(n) = s^{3}/n^{2}"}</Math>, feasibility <Math>{"c \\ge n"}</Math> is exactly <Math>{"n \\le s"}</Math>, and <Math>{"n^{*} > s"}</Math> in this regime, so <Math>{"C"}</Math> is strictly decreasing across the whole feasible interval and the minimum sits at its right endpoint <Math>{"n = c = s"}</Math>, giving <Math>{"C^{*} = (\\delta + g)\\,s"}</Math>. At <Math>{"\\delta = 2g"}</Math> the corner and interior solutions coincide at <Math>{"n = c = s"}</Math> and both expressions give <Math>{"3g\\,s"}</Math>, so <Math>{"\\gamma"}</Math> is continuous. Neither branch depends on <Math>{"s"}</Math>, so <Math>{"C^{*}"}</Math> is exactly linear in <Math>{"s"}</Math> in each. Finally, the optimizing <Math>{"n"}</Math> in each branch is <Math>{"s"}</Math> and <Math>{"s(2g/\\delta)^{1/3}"}</Math> respectively, and requiring it to satisfy the floor <Math>{"n \\ge d_{\\min}"}</Math> is exactly <Math>{"s \\ge s_{0}"}</Math>. <Math>{"\\square"}</Math>
                    </Proof>
                    <p>
                        The regimes read plainly. When identities are cheap relative to trade (<Math>{"\\delta \\le 2g"}</Math>), the adversary buys pure breadth, one process per fabricated seller, because under the cube root a second process from one seller is worth less than a first from a new one. When identities are dear (<Math>{"\\delta > 2g"}</Math>), gas-bought volume substitutes for them, at <Math>{"(\\delta/2g)^{2/3}"}</Math> processes per identity. Below <Math>{"s_{0}"}</Math> the floor binds before either optimum is reachable, so small fabricated scores cost strictly more than <Math>{"\\gamma s"}</Math>, and the linear figure is the conservative one to quote.
                    </p>
                    <PaperRemark title="Linearity is the property.">
                        The result worth holding is the shape, not the constant: fabricated score has a flat unit price, and there is no scale at which the adversary&rsquo;s outlay buys more than proportionally. Linearity is the direct consequence of counting breadth in the unit the stake prices. Under (buyer, seller) pairs it fails outright: one staked seller and <Math>{"N"}</Math> free buyer wallets yield <Math>{"N"}</Math> units of breadth, so <Math>{"C^{*}"}</Math> would be bounded by a constant in <Math>{"\\delta"}</Math> however large the score, and no stake or cooldown could repair it.
                    </PaperRemark>
                    <PaperRemark title="The stake has diminishing returns.">
                        Raising the stake does not raise the adversary&rsquo;s price indefinitely. Past <Math>{"\\delta = 2g"}</Math>, <Math>{"\\gamma"}</Math> grows only as <Math>{"\\delta^{2/3}"}</Math>, because gas-bought volume substitutes for identities under the cube root: doubling the stake there raises the unit price by about 59%, not 100%. Two consequences follow. Below saturation the cooldown is the stronger lever, since <Math>{"\\delta"}</Math> is linear in <Math>{"T/P"}</Math> until it reaches <Math>{"D"}</Math>. And no stake substitutes for measuring <Math>{"g"}</Math> on the cheapest route, since <Math>{"g"}</Math> sets the floor the stake works against.
                    </PaperRemark>
                </PaperSubsection>
                <PaperSubsection title="7.3 Dilution: what an outlay buys of a period&rsquo;s budget">
                    <p>
                        The model rests on four hypotheses, stated first because the result is worth exactly what they are. Honest accrual <Math>{"S_h"}</Math> is exogenous: it is whatever genuine adopters counted, and does not respond to the adversary. The channel is dilution only; the seller-withdrawal exposure of Section 4, which drops accrual honest designers would have earned, is a suppression channel outside this model. Counts are relaxed to the reals, so the figures are exact over that relaxation and integrality rounds the adversary&rsquo;s outlay up. And one denominator is in view: the period&rsquo;s total score across both routes, which is what a claim divides by.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        A wallet&rsquo;s reward is its score over the period&rsquo;s total, so the adversary&rsquo;s real objective is a share. Let it want a fraction <Math>{"\\varphi \\in (0,1)"}</Math> of the period&rsquo;s budget. Its own score <Math>{"s_A"}</Math> enters the denominator too, so it needs <Math>{"s_A/(s_A + S_h) = \\varphi"}</Math>, that is <Math>{"s_A = S_h\\,\\varphi/(1-\\varphi)"}</Math>. With the Proposition:
                    </p>
                    <div className="my-3 overflow-x-auto">
                        <Math display>{"C(\\varphi) \;=\; \\gamma\\,S_h\\,\\frac{\\varphi}{1-\\varphi}\\,, \\qquad \\frac{dC}{d\\varphi} \;=\; \\frac{\\gamma\\,S_h}{(1-\\varphi)^{2}} \;>\; 0."}</Math>
                    </div>
                    <p>
                        The curve is convex and unbounded as <Math>{"\\varphi \\to 1"}</Math>, and three things follow. First, honest designers always keep a positive share, because the last increment of share costs without limit. Second, the marginal price of share rises with the share already taken: an adversary at a tenth spends about 23% more for the next increment than for the first, and one at half spends four times as much, so capture gets harder as it proceeds. Third, the statement is denominated in outlay per unit of dilution, never in profit. The mechanism sets no price for the florin and reads none, so a claim that an attack is unprofitable below some stake would smuggle in a price forecast. What it can publish exactly is the cost side, and a reader supplies its own view of the other.
                    </p>
                    <PaperRun title="The entry price, and where the worst case sits.">
                        The floor truncates the curve from the left. A registration scores nothing until <Math>{"d_{\\min}"}</Math> distinct staked sellers carry it, so the smallest score the mechanism recognizes is <Math>{"d_{\\min}"}</Math>, the curve begins at <Math>{"\\varphi_{0} = d_{\\min}/(d_{\\min} + S_h)"}</Math>, and the entry price is <Math>{"d_{\\min}(\\delta + g)"}</Math>: three identity-periods and three resolved processes at the reference floor. That is at or above <Math>{"\\gamma\\,d_{\\min}"}</Math>, because a floor-bound adversary buys identities the unconstrained optimum would not. One actor can pay it; the floor sets the entry price and keeps one- and two-identity noise out of the denominator. Since <Math>{"\\varphi_{0}"}</Math> is measured against <Math>{"S_h"}</Math>, the worst case is the first period, where <Math>{"S_h"}</Math> is smallest. The schedule answers it: the first period disposes of 7.5% of the reserve, the smallest budget the schedule releases and barely more than two-thirds of an equal ninth, while each of the last four disposes of 13.75%. The period in which fabricated score buys the largest share is the one with the least at stake.
                    </PaperRun>
                    <PaperRemark title="How the curve moves with the stake and the cooldown.">
                        The stake and the cooldown enter only through <Math>{"\\delta"}</Math>, and <Math>{"\\delta"}</Math> only through <Math>{"\\gamma"}</Math>, which multiplies the whole curve. Raising the stake therefore scales <Math>{"C(\\varphi)"}</Math> vertically and leaves its convexity, its divergence and the ratio of the price of any two shares untouched. The scaling is inelastic: <Math>{"d\\ln\\gamma/d\\ln\\delta = \\delta/(\\delta+g)"}</Math> in the first regime, rising to <Math>{"\\tfrac23"}</Math> at <Math>{"\\delta = 2g"}</Math> and holding there in the second, so the elasticity of capture price to the stake is at most two-thirds everywhere. Below saturation the stake and the cooldown are interchangeable, since every pair holding <Math>{"D\\,T/P"}</Math> fixed lands on the same curve; at <Math>{"T \\ge P"}</Math> only <Math>{"D"}</Math> moves anything. We characterize the movement and stop: nothing in the mechanism optimizes over its own constants.
                    </PaperRemark>
                    <PaperRun title="What the bound prices, and what it does not.">
                        The Proposition prices score on one route and one registration. Across the two routes the same fabricated sellers, carrying half their processes on each, score up to <Math>{"2^{2/3} \\approx 1.59"}</Math> times the pooled figure at no further identity cost (Section 4). That figure assumes an equal split and the same <Math>{"g"}</Math> on both routes; under those hypotheses the cross-route unit price is <Math>{"\\gamma \\cdot 2^{-2/3}"}</Math>, a lower bound, since any other split or a dearer leg prices it higher, and the shape stays linear in <Math>{"s"}</Math>. The floor is untouched: reaching it still takes <Math>{"d_{\\min}"}</Math> distinct staked identities on each route. An adversary who registers several clauses and commits all of them in the same fabricated agreements replicates the score across each. The identity and resolution outlay is shared, while each replicate costs its own designer-side registration stake plus, on the direct route, one further counting call per process, or on the batched route one further per-key accrual write per batch, and that stake cannot be recycled: a registration binds permanently, its stake can be withdrawn once, and it must stay live for the registration to earn. The bound is therefore per registration and per route, never over a portfolio, and the designer-side stake is what prices replication. Nor does the bound price collusion among genuine participants. An adversary who persuades real staked sellers to carry a junk clause beside their real work has bought breadth at the price of persuasion, and no rule reading only chain state can tell that from adoption; the clause is scored on what it demonstrably carried, like every other.
                    </PaperRun>
                </PaperSubsection>
                <PaperSubsection title="7.4 What a reader can recompute">
                    <p>
                        The stakes, the cooldown and the period length are constants fixed at deployment, and with the measured <Math>{"g"}</Math> they fix the point the mechanism occupies on the <Math>{"C(\\varphi)"}</Math> curve. Publishing them beside the formula for <Math>{"\\gamma"}</Math> makes &ldquo;how was the stake sized?&rdquo; a question with an arithmetic answer: a reader takes the constants from the deployed contracts, computes <Math>{"\\delta"}</Math> and <Math>{"\\gamma"}</Math>, estimates <Math>{"S_h"}</Math> from the period&rsquo;s public accrual, and reads the outlay of any dilution off the curve.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The price does not buy impossibility: every share short of the whole is purchasable at a finite figure. What the mechanism holds against fabricated adoption is four structural facts. Fabrication costs a published, recomputable outlay per unit of score. That outlay must stay committed while the reward it gates is earned, on both sides and by different clocks. The reserve is fixed, so a fabricator dilutes a budget it cannot enlarge. And every counted process costs gas, spent whoever sends it, which is the one cost that scales with fabricated volume.
                    </p>
                </PaperSubsection>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="8. Uniform Pro-Rata Distribution and Designer Eligibility">
                <p>
                    A wallet claims a closed period once, presenting in that one call each registration it is the designer of record for, each named as a pair of its family and its key and listed once. Each pair is checked against its family&rsquo;s registry and no other, so the list is a lookup key, never an assertion of ownership. A withdrawn registration or a repeated pair reverts the whole claim rather than scoring zero within it. The family is part of the key because nothing in 32 bytes tells a clause, a registered agreement and an assembly apart, and the registries hold no edge between them. A wallet can register an existing assembly&rsquo;s root as a clause key, and that registration earns what the clause family counts under the key, the processes whose agreements commit it as a clause, and nothing of the assembly&rsquo;s count. The scores of the presented registrations are summed into one quantity, and the wallet&rsquo;s share of the period&rsquo;s budget is that quantity over the period&rsquo;s total score, minted to it directly, with no per-wallet ceiling.
                </p>
                <PaperRun title="Designer eligibility: the stake must be live.">
                    A designer earns for a registration only while the stake placed at its registration remains unwithdrawn, and the check runs at both ends: no count naming the registration lands after the stake is withdrawn (Section 4), and a withdrawn registration cannot be presented at a claim. The adoption counted for it while its stake was live goes unclaimed, and the forfeited share is not redistributed: the score stays in the period&rsquo;s total, and the share goes unminted, as flooring dust does. This closes the path of registering, farming and withdrawing, because the stake that makes a registration costly cannot be recovered while the registration still earns. Since a registration binds permanently and its stake can be withdrawn exactly once, nothing can be placed again against it, so each replicated registration of Section 7.3 costs a fresh stake rather than the same stake cycled.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Why there is no cap.">
                    The mechanism places no ceiling on any wallet&rsquo;s share. A cap would defend against a concentration the two-sided stake already prices: a wallet reaches a large share only by carrying a large amount of resolved, broad adoption across live-staked sellers, and fabricating that costs the published unit price of Section 7 against a reserve it can only dilute. A cap would add an arbitrary fraction to defend and leave the clamped remainder unminted. Uniform pro rata tracks use directly, and the wallet that broadened the substrate most receives the most. A period&rsquo;s budget is a ceiling, not a target: flooring dust stays unminted, and nothing can sweep what a period did not mint.
                </PaperRun>
                <p>
                    Nothing in the procedure requires knowing who else is claiming. The denominator is a number the counting contract already holds and can no longer change, and the numerator is the claimant&rsquo;s own live registrations.
                </p>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="9. Counting Adoption as It Happens">
                <p>
                    The arithmetic above can be trusted because no claim about the past is ever stated, not because one is posted, contested and adjudicated. The design problem was never how to check an allocator&rsquo;s claim; it was how to need none.
                </p>
                <PaperRun title="The chain cannot look backwards.">
                    Two properties of the chain combine into one constraint. The Core calls no registry and notifies no other contract of a resolution, and that separation is deliberate: coupling resolution to a registry would make the registries load-bearing for trade. And no contract can read an event, because logs are an interface for observers outside the machine, not state inside it. So when a process resolves, the fact that it used a particular clause is on the chain as data no contract can later consult. A mechanism that rewards adoption must either reconstruct that fact after the event or capture it at the event, and everything about the two designs follows from that fork.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Reconstruction forces a chain of consequences.">
                    Take the first branch and each step forces the next. Reconstruction happens off chain, since the chain cannot do it, so the answer must be brought back on chain: somebody <em>posts</em> it. A posting is an unverified claim about the past, so it must be made costly with a <em>bond</em>. A bond deters only if a false posting can be attacked, which takes a <em>challenge</em>. A challenge puts bonds on both sides, and something must decide who keeps them, which takes a <em>forum</em>. The allocation has now acquired a dispute layer, an escalation path, a concession deadline and an outside adjudicator to compose and trust, and none of it rewards a single public good.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Capture removes the claim.">
                    Take the second branch and the sequence never starts. A count names a resolved order and a registration, and the counting contract checks both halves against state the chain already holds: it recomputes the order hash and requires the Core to report it resolved, and it requires the registration&rsquo;s inclusion under the merkle root the order carries. Neither check consults the caller. On the batched route the same facts are carried by the batch&rsquo;s proof, and the sequencer that submits the batch carries only what each party signed; what stands in for a caller&rsquo;s word there is an assumption about a proof system, not about a party. Nothing is posted, so nothing is bonded, challenged or adjudicated. The apparatus is not simplified; it is unnecessary.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Periods, not snapshots.">
                    One difficulty survives. If a budget were shared pro rata out of a total that kept growing, early claimants would take more than their share, and the usual answer is a snapshot: a checkpoint of every score at a chosen block, walked at claim time. The mechanism takes none. Accrual falls into fixed periods set when the counting contract is deployed, each period&rsquo;s budget rewards that period alone, and a claim requires the period to have ended. A late count lands in whatever period is open, never in a closed one, so a closed period&rsquo;s counters and total are immovable. A claimant reads a denominator no transaction can change, with no checkpoint, no history walk and no block height for anyone to choose. Claims never expire, because a closed period&rsquo;s arithmetic is stable forever.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Crediting a registered agreement and an assembly.">
                    What a count proves is that a 32-byte key was committed in the signed agreement, and the count is kept under the family it names. For a clause the key is the leaf key itself, the hash of its specification. A registered agreement and an assembly are committed one step differently, through the provenance leaf. Its key is a constant of the format, and its committed bytes are fully determined by two hashes: the agreement key the order instantiated and the root of the assembly that holds it. A wrong key derives a leaf that is not in the tree. Proving the provenance leaf credits the registered agreement&rsquo;s designer, by an inclusion proof of the same shape that credits a clause&rsquo;s. The assembly&rsquo;s designer needs one proof more. The assembly&rsquo;s root is the merkle root over one leaf per registered agreement and, when present, its assembly terms leaf, so the agreement&rsquo;s membership in the assembly is a membership proof against the registered root, never a lookup. There is no linkage table and no inversion step. Fixing the provenance leaf&rsquo;s key in the format, rather than letting a caller name the leaf it proves, is what stops a clause whose bytes happen to name a root from standing in for it.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Claiming, and the two budget backstops.">
                    A claim mints directly to the claimant, with nothing held between, no root to prove against, and nothing that expires. Issuance is bounded twice. The minter tracks what it has minted per period and refuses any claim that would overspend that period&rsquo;s budget. The token independently caps what this minter may ever mint at the 600M reserve, a cap fixed when the minter was registered, which no party can raise. The second bound holds even if the first were wrong.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="The rule is the code.">
                    The exponents, the floor <Math>{"d_{\\min}"}</Math>, the two live-stake gates, the reserved keys, the nine period boundaries and the nine budgets are all constants of the deployed contracts, storage written at construction with no setter, or first-write-wins registry state. The two schedules cannot be deployed inconsistently, because the minter rejects a budget list whose length differs from the counter&rsquo;s period count. There is no pause, no upgrade path and no configuration surface; changing any constant means deploying a new token system. This is stronger than anchoring a document&rsquo;s hash on chain, because no document exists that could disagree with what executes.
                </PaperRun>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="10. Why Reconstruction-Based Verification Fails">
                <p>
                    Reconstruction admits two natural approaches, and neither escapes the fork of Section 9: the bond, the challenge and the forum are a cost of the branch, not of a technique. We work through both, because the case for capture is made by what reconstruction costs.
                </p>
                <PaperRun title="Proof-gated verification.">
                    One approach gates a posted root behind a zero-knowledge proof: a succinct-proof virtual machine runs the aggregator and attests that the root is the formula&rsquo;s output over a supplied event window. The proof binds the <em>formula</em> to the <em>inputs</em>: given those events, the root is exactly the formula&rsquo;s output. It cannot bind the inputs to <em>chain history</em>. A submitter who feeds the prover a doctored event stream, omitting some clauses&rsquo; use and inventing others&rsquo;, obtains a valid proof of the wrong allocation, because the proof certifies computation and says nothing of provenance. Input provenance remains a trusted-submitter assumption, so the approach pays for proving without discharging the branch&rsquo;s cost.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRemark title="Why proved batches are not this case.">
                    A batch&rsquo;s proof does carry accrual, and the difference is the one the reconstruction approach fails to make. What the batch proof counts is a function of the state the batch itself advances, under a verifying key fixed at deployment, so there is no window to choose and no stream to doctor: the counted set rides the same proven state transition that resolves the trade. The proof discharges the three conditions of Section 4 that concern the trade: that the order resolved, that the registration was committed in the signed agreement, and that the process has not been counted for it before. The counting call checks what the proof cannot see, the live chain state: the seller&rsquo;s stake, the registration&rsquo;s stake and the open period; it also skips the reserved keys, format constants the proof already refuses. Reconstruction fails because it proves a computation over inputs whose provenance nothing establishes; here the input is the proven state transition itself.
                </PaperRemark>
                <PaperRun title="Posted root under a bonded challenge.">
                    A second approach drops the proof and fixes the window by the chain instead: every event up to the block before the posting, the same for every observer. It then applies the pattern optimistic constructions are built on, in the shape Kalodner et al. (2018) set out for off-chain virtual-machine execution: a claim about a computation stands unless a party disputes it, and a challenge protocol identifies and penalizes the party that lied. A poster locks a bond, anyone may lock an equal bond to void the posting, and an unchallenged posting finalizes. This closes the provenance hole and is sound on its own terms. Its cost is the sequence of Section 9. A correct allocation waits on a challenge period whose only function is to give a hypothetical challenger time. The bonds at stake in a challenge need a rule, which means an escalation path, a concession deadline and an arbitration forum composed into the token system, a discretionary body inside a mechanism whose claim is to have none. And safety rests on liveness: at least one honest party watching, recomputing and willing to bond at each of nine annual postings.
                </PaperRun>
                <p>
                    Counting as it happens dominates both by refusing their shared premise. There is no window, because there is no reconstruction; no root, because there is no assertion; and no bond, challenge, forum or watcher, so a correct allocation is claimable when its period closes and an incorrect one cannot be expressed. When a construction accumulates a bond, a challenge and a referee, the thing to examine is the claim it was built to make believable.
                </p>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="11. Scope">
                <PaperRun title="Cohort selection is the formula.">
                    The recipient set is the designers of registrations with eligible adoption in the period: counted while the seller&rsquo;s and the registration&rsquo;s stakes were live, scoring above the floor, and claimed while the registration&rsquo;s stake is live. The proportions are the rule&rsquo;s output, with no list to curate and no committee to convene. The mechanism therefore rewards exactly three kinds of contribution, clause design, agreement composition and assembly design, and is silent on every other kind of protocol work: client development, documentation, review, operations. Whether to fund those from its treasury is the DAO&rsquo;s judgment.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="Single denomination per process.">
                    The Core binds each process to a single denomination, and the counting layer relies on none: it reads a process&rsquo;s parties and its resolution, compares no values across tokens, and never writes to the Core. What is scored is the shape of a trade, not the units it moved.
                </PaperRun>
                <PaperRun title="The rule optimizes nothing.">
                    The mechanism has no social-welfare objective. A welfare criterion over designers would rank kinds of contribution, a ranking justifies weights, and a weight is the judgment Section 6 declines. Neutrality comes from holding no objective over kinds of work: the reward is uniform, and the two-sided stake carries what the score cannot. The constants are published as calibrations of that position: the exponents a judgment about breadth over depth, the floor the number of separately priced identities a registration must gather, and the stakes, the cooldown and the period length a point on a curve any reader can recompute.
                </PaperRun>
            </PaperSection>

            <PaperSection title="12. Conclusion">
                <p>
                    A designer reward can rest on facts the chain already holds, and nothing else. Because a resolution is the buyer&rsquo;s terminal acceptance, a count of resolved processes measures completed trade; because the count is checked at the event against the signed merkle root and the Core&rsquo;s status, no allocator, submitter, challenger or referee is needed to make it believable; and because breadth is counted in distinct staked sellers, the one quantity the score rewards most is the one a stake prices. The result is a Sybil resistance stated as a price: fabricated score has a flat unit price in the base currency, a share of a period costs an outlay convex in that share and unbounded as it approaches the whole, and every figure is recomputable from the deployed constants and one gas measurement. Designers of clauses, registered agreements and assemblies are rewarded for what their work demonstrably carried, and nothing between the trade and the reward holds an opinion.
                </p>
            </PaperSection>
        </PaperLayout>
    );
}
